column16:
The 2030 Nature Plan is the framework policy for biodiversity conservation in Québec. It was developed by the Government of Québec following the adoption of the Kunming-Montreal Global Biodiversity Framework at the 15th Conference of the Parties to the UN Convention on Biological Diversity (COP15), held in Montréal in Dec.2022. It aims to encourage participation in meeting global targets, while taking into consideration Québec's territorial realities, competencies and priorities in this area.

It sets out a vision for 2030 supported by four key foundations for its deployment:
› involve the First Nations and the Inuit;
› mobilize society as a whole; 
› promote discussion and synergy among stakeholders concerned with biodiversity;
› improve access to nature, in particular for its health benefits.

The 2030 Nature Plan is broken down into three areas of action (goals) and 14 targets that are aligned with the global targets of the Global Biodiversity Framework. Its implementation is supported by an Action Plan covering the period 2024-2028 that comprises 33 objectives. Structured monitoring of the action plan's progress will be carried out in partnership with the parties involved and made public in the financial year 2025-2026.
